> Can I create a leaflet without passing in a shapefile? While your renderLeaflet call adds polygons, mine just adds the tiles. Then I add the polygons later. So I don't want to add the tiles again, but currently the use of shapefile_data() means that renderLeaflet is called every time shapefile_data is changed.
**assistant**:
> Yes, you can definitely create a `leaflet` map without initially passing in a shapefile. You can start by creating a map with just the base layer (tiles) and add your polygons later. This can help you avoid re-rendering the entire map when only the polygons change, thus keeping your app performant.
> 
> ### Suggested Approach
> 
> 1. **Initialize the Leaflet Map**: In the `renderLeaflet` function, you can create the leaflet map with just the tile layer and no polygons.
>   
> 2. **Use `leafletProxy` to Update Polygons**: When you want to add or update the polygons, you can use `leafletProxy` to manipulate the existing map, which allows you to avoid re-rendering the base layer.
> 
> Here’s how you might set this up:
> 
> ```r
> library(shiny)
> library(leaflet)
> library(sf)
> 
> ui <- fluidPage(
>   leafletOutput("mymap"),
>   actionButton("update_map", "Update Map")
> )
> 
> server <- function(input, output, session) {
>   # Create an empty reactive value for the shapefile data
>   shapefile_data <- reactiveVal(NULL)
> 
>   # Render the initial leaflet map with only tiles
>   output$mymap <- renderLeaflet({
>     leaflet() %>%
>       addTiles()  # Only adds the base layer at start
>   })
> 
>   # Update or load a new shapefile
>   observeEvent(input$update_map, {
>     # Load new shapefile
>     new_data <- st_read("path/to/your/new/shapefile.shp")  # Make sure this path is correct
>     shapefile_data(new_data)  # Store the new shapefile data
> 
>     # Use leafletProxy to add polygons without re-rendering the base map
>     leafletProxy("mymap", session) %>%
>       clearShapes() %>%
>       addPolygons(data = shapefile_data())  # Add the polygons to the map
>   })
> }
> 
> shinyApp(ui, server)
> ```
> 
> ### Key Points:
> 
> 1. **Initial Leaflet Map**: The `renderLeaflet` creates the base map with `addTiles()` and does not reference the shapefile data initially.
> 
> 2. **Reactive Value**: The `shapefile_data` is set as a reactive value but is not needed in the initial map render.
> 
> 3. **Updating the Map**: When you press the "Update Map" button, it loads the new shapefile and uses `leafletProxy()` to clear existing shapes and then add the new polygons, avoiding the need to re-render the entire map, which enhances performance.
> 
> ### Why This Works:
> 
> - By using `leafletProxy`, you interact directly with the previously rendered leaflet object, allowing for efficient updates.
> - This method keeps the UI responsive, as only the necessary components are updated without unnecessary re-renders.
> 
> Feel free to adapt the logic and let me know if you have any further questions!
